    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-13305",
      "problem": "Suppose $n=10-30p,$ where $p \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is even.",
      "worked_solution": "An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2a,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is even since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =10-30p \\\\  & =2({{5-15p}_{⏟}}_{a}) \\\\  & =2a,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5-15p},$ which is an integer.",
